IP Address : A unique string of numbers identifying a specific device on a network It tells the internet exactly which computer our request needs to travel to
DNS - Domain Name System we cannot easily memorize numerical IP addresses. DNS acts as the internet's phonebook. When you type google.com, a DNS server instantly translates that text domain into its raw numerical IP address 
Port  A single computer running on an IP address can run dozens of network applications at the same time. A Port is a specific communication channel it varies from 0 to 65535 that routes traffic to a specific software program
	
HTTP - Hypertext Transfer Protocol (The Language): Once our browser connects to the correct computer (IP) and application (Port), they must speak the same language. HTTP is the structured text protocol they use to chat.


Request and Response
our browser constructs an HTTP request packet containing:
A Method  GET (fetch data), POST (submit data), PUT (update data), or DELETE (remove data).
Headers: Metadata containing configuration rules 
payload data 
Response 
After processing the request, the server returns an HTTP response packet containing:
Status Code
Headers: Metadata about the file coming back 
Body: The actual file contents (the HTML text, image data, or API payload).


Static Site Generation (SSG)
SSG is the third major web architecture.
SSG compiles all your pages into flat, raw HTML files exactly once—during the build step (before the app ever goes live).
The SSG Lifecycle
when we type npm run build. The framework hits the database, pulls down all information, and renders hundreds of structural flat files onto your storage drive.
You upload these finished static files directly to a globally distributed network called a CDN (Content Delivery Network).
User Request: A user visits our site. The CDN doesn't run any database operations or server-side calculations; it instantly hands back the pre-built, cached HTML file.



